Overview

Tor Onion Network is a project for documenting legitimate Tor networking, secure client configuration, and access to authorized onion services from a Linux environment.

The project focuses on:

- Connecting a Linux system to the Tor network.
- Understanding how ".onion" services work.
- Configuring a local Tor SOCKS proxy.
- Testing connectivity and diagnosing common issues.
- Applying security, privacy, and operational best practices.

This repository provides configuration guidance and documentation. It does not claim to be a running Tor network, a hosted onion service, or an anonymous hosting provider.

How It Works

Tor routes supported network traffic through a distributed network of relays. Onion services are accessed through Tor without requiring a conventional public IP address for the service.

Linux Cloud Server
        |
        v
     Tor Client
        |
        v
   Local SOCKS5
    127.0.0.1:9050
        |
        v
     Tor Network
        |
        v
Authorized Onion Service

The diagram illustrates the intended client-side architecture. A working connection requires a properly installed Tor client and a reachable destination.

Requirements

Before beginning, prepare:

- A Linux cloud server or virtual machine.
- SSH access and an account with appropriate administrative privileges.
- A supported Linux distribution, such as Debian or Ubuntu.
- Internet access for connecting to the Tor network.
- The Tor software from a trusted distribution or the official Tor Project.
- A valid destination address obtained from a trustworthy source.

Important: A cloud server is not automatically a Tor-enabled browser. This setup provides a Tor client and a local SOCKS proxy for compatible applications.

Installation — Debian / Ubuntu

1. Update the system

sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade

2. Install Tor and curl

sudo apt install tor curl

For production deployments, follow the official Tor Project installation guidance if you need a particular supported Tor version.

3. Start the Tor service

sudo systemctl enable --now tor

Check the service status:

systemctl status tor --no-pager

If the service fails, inspect its logs:

sudo journalctl -u tor -n 50 --no-pager

Package names and service behavior can vary by distribution.

Configure and Test Client Connectivity

The following example assumes a Tor SOCKS proxy is listening on "127.0.0.1:9050".

Check whether the local port is listening:

ss -ltn | grep ':9050'

If the port is not listening, inspect the active Tor configuration and service logs before proceeding.

Test Tor connectivity using the Tor Project's check endpoint:

curl --fail --show-error \
  --socks5-hostname 127.0.0.1:9050 \
  https://check.torproject.org/api/ip

The "--socks5-hostname" option allows hostname resolution through the SOCKS proxy instead of resolving the destination hostname locally.

A successful request confirms that this request reached the check endpoint through Tor. It does not prove that every application on the server is using Tor.

Accessing Onion Services

Onion services use addresses ending in ".onion" and require a Tor-compatible client.

For command-line testing, use an onion address that you own or are authorized to access:

curl --socks5-hostname 127.0.0.1:9050 \
  --connect-timeout 30 \
  --max-time 60 \
  http://YOUR_AUTHORIZED_ADDRESS.onion/

Replace the placeholder with the actual address. This is a connectivity example, not a real service address.

For interactive browsing, the official Tor Browser is generally the recommended option. A headless Linux server does not automatically provide its graphical interface.

Only use legitimate services and respect applicable laws, access controls, and service rules.

Security Best Practices

Protect the server

- Keep the operating system and Tor package updated.
- Use SSH keys where appropriate and restrict administrative access.
- Configure the cloud firewall to expose only necessary services.
- Keep the SOCKS proxy bound to loopback unless a separately secured architecture explicitly requires otherwise.
- Never expose an unrestricted Tor SOCKS proxy directly to the public internet.
- Do not store passwords, private keys, or access tokens in GitHub.

Protect privacy

- Tor does not make every application or server configuration anonymous.
- Applications can leak information through their own requests, logs, or configuration.
- Do not assume that using Tor makes downloaded files or untrusted content safe.
- Use HTTPS for ordinary websites whenever available.
- Treat server-provider records, operating-system logs, and account activity as separate privacy considerations.

Protect project secrets

Never commit private keys, authentication credentials, real user data, or sensitive server configuration to this repository.

If credentials are accidentally published, revoke or rotate them promptly. Deleting a file from the latest commit may not remove it from the repository's history.

Troubleshooting

Problem| Recommended check
Tor service will not start| Check "systemctl status tor" and system logs
Port 9050 is unavailable| Inspect the effective Tor configuration and listening sockets
Connection times out| Check server internet access, firewall rules, and Tor bootstrap status
Onion address cannot resolve| Verify the address and ensure hostname resolution is routed through Tor
Website does not respond| Check service availability and whether authentication is required
Unexpected privacy exposure| Review proxy settings, application behavior, server logs, and firewall rules

Do not disable security controls or expose the proxy publicly just to resolve a connectivity issue.

Project Scope

This repository is intended for:

- Learning about Tor networking.
- Configuring a Tor client on Linux.
- Testing authorized onion-service connectivity.
- Documenting secure cloud-server configuration.
- Developing future open-source networking utilities.

It is not intended to host illegal content, bypass legitimate access controls, or guarantee complete anonymity.

